DEBUG: publish the address of one table_ident slot, for a watchpoint.

table_ident[199] -- the TokenSym for __multf3 -- is overwritten between
interning and tccpp_delete by a stray eight-byte write. Its neighbours 198 and
200 survive, so it is one slot, not a bulk corruption. Ruled out by
measurement: the tal allocator, the hash chain, realloc's copy, allocation
overlap, sym_link, and parse_btype's TOK_LONG path. Bisection with write()
markers narrowed it to "between two tokens, while parsing the alloca prototype
at <command line>:95" and then stopped narrowing.

A watchpoint answers "which instruction wrote this address" directly, and it
needs the address. That address is a heap pointer, known only at runtime, so
the watchpoint job runs the binary twice: once to read this line off stderr,
once under gdb watching what it printed. The layout repeats because there is no
ASLR difference between the two runs and M2libc's allocator is a straight brk
bump; if that ever stops holding the symptom is a watchpoint that never fires,
which is loud.

IT CANNOT BE DONE UNDER THE EMULATOR. qemu-user's gdbstub was checked packet by
packet: Z0 and Z1 return OK, Z2/Z3/Z4 return empty. Watchpoints live in QEMU's
system-mode path. So the job runs natively on the aarch64 runner, which is what
that runner is for.

This targets tccpp.c, which patches 0001 and 0002 do not touch, so it applies
independently of them.

write() and a hand-rolled formatter, not printf: stdio may not be usable this
early and a buffer that is never flushed loses exactly the line being
collected. The D markers in the driver use write() for the same reason, and
that reason is written down there too.

diff --git a/tccpp.c b/tccpp.c
index 5a5ee07..fcac69e 100644
--- a/tccpp.c
+++ b/tccpp.c
@@ -477,6 +477,33 @@ static TokenSym *tok_alloc_new(TokenSym **pts, const char *str, int len)
 
     ts = tal_realloc(&toksym_alloc, 0, sizeof(TokenSym) + len);
     table_ident[i] = ts;
+    /* DEBUG: publish the address of one table slot, for a watchpoint.
+     *
+     * table_ident[199] is the TokenSym for __multf3, and it is being
+     * overwritten between interning and tccpp_delete by a stray eight-byte
+     * write -- its neighbours 198 and 200 survive. Bisection with markers
+     * narrowed that to "between two tokens while parsing the alloca prototype
+     * in the predefs" and then stopped narrowing.
+     *
+     * A watchpoint answers it directly, and needs the address. It is a heap
+     * pointer, so it is only knowable at runtime: this prints it, the
+     * watchpoint job reads it from stderr on a first pass and watches it on a
+     * second. Under qemu-user that is impossible -- its gdbstub refuses
+     * Z2/Z3/Z4 -- so the job runs natively on the aarch64 runner.
+     *
+     * write() and a hand-rolled formatter because stdio may not be usable at
+     * this point and a buffer that is never flushed loses the one line that
+     * matters. Same reason the D markers in the driver use write(). */
+    if (i == 199) {
+        char d[24]; int j = 0; unsigned long x = (unsigned long)&table_ident[199];
+        write(2, "TW-armed at=", 12);
+        if (x == 0) write(2, "0", 1);
+        else {
+            while (x) { d[j] = 48 + (int)(x % 10); j = j + 1; x = x / 10; }
+            while (j) { j = j - 1; write(2, d + j, 1); }
+        }
+        write(2, "\n", 1);
+    }
     ts->tok = tok_ident++;
     ts->sym_define = NULL;
     ts->sym_label = NULL;
